WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.147 --> 00:00:02.164
- [Voiceover] You probably
recognize these ice cream pellets

00:00:02.164 --> 00:00:04.599
as the ice cream of the future.

00:00:04.599 --> 00:00:06.861
They're Dippin’ Dots, a summertime staple.

00:00:06.861 --> 00:00:08.419
But this confectionery treat

00:00:08.419 --> 00:00:11.114
didn't start as, well, ice cream.

00:00:11.114 --> 00:00:13.031
It started as cow feed.

00:00:17.303 --> 00:00:19.008
Dippin’ Dots were invented in the 80s,

00:00:19.008 --> 00:00:22.824
not by an ice cream brand
but by a microbiologist.

00:00:22.824 --> 00:00:25.766
Curtis Jones specialized in cryogenics.

00:00:25.766 --> 00:00:28.882
In 1987, he was working
for a bio-tech company

00:00:28.882 --> 00:00:31.489
in Kentucky, trying to
figure out how to make food

00:00:31.489 --> 00:00:33.999
for farm animals more efficient.

00:00:33.999 --> 00:00:36.905
His big breakthrough came when
he flash-froze cattle feed

00:00:36.905 --> 00:00:41.847
at 350 degrees below zero,
which produced small pellets.

00:00:41.847 --> 00:00:45.319
Serendipitously, Curtis
loved making ice cream.

00:00:45.319 --> 00:00:47.816
Next thing he knew, he
was using liquid nitrogen

00:00:47.816 --> 00:00:50.739
to freeze ice cream at
extremely low temperatures

00:00:50.739 --> 00:00:52.733
and ended up with small beads of it.

00:00:52.733 --> 00:00:55.887
When eaten, the natural heat
of the mouth melted the beads

00:00:55.887 --> 00:00:58.470
and thus, Dippin’ Dots was born.

00:00:59.486 --> 00:01:01.896
A year later, he formed the
company out of his parent's

00:01:01.896 --> 00:01:04.586
garage in Illinois, but
there was a problem.

00:01:04.586 --> 00:01:06.747
Curtis had nowhere to sell the product.

00:01:06.747 --> 00:01:09.470
Dippin’ Dots need to be stored
at such a cold temperature

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:11.568
that it made it impossible
for grocery stores

00:01:11.568 --> 00:01:13.923
to house the tasty
treat, so he got creative

00:01:13.923 --> 00:01:16.776
and marketed his product
to alternate locations.

00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:20.055
Now, they're sold at amusement
parks, festivals, zoos,

00:01:20.055 --> 00:01:22.615
and other summertime destinations.

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:24.399
But whether or not they
really are the ice cream

00:01:24.399 --> 00:01:28.316
of the future, we'll just
have to wait and see.

